course: world
lesson: 118
title: Day and night, shadows and sundials
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The Sun does not go anywhere at night. The Earth spins once about every 24 hours, like a ball spun on a finger, and the half facing the Sun has day while the other half has night. Sunrise is your side turning towards the Sun; sunset is your side turning away.
explain: Shadows show where the Sun is. At midday the Sun is highest and your shadow is short and stubby; in the morning and evening the Sun is low and your shadow stretches out like a giant's.
explain: A sundial is a stick, called a gnomon, that throws a shadow onto marks; as the Earth turns, the shadow creeps round like a clock hand. It tells Sun time, so in British summer time it reads an hour behind the watch, and on a cloudy day it tells nothing at all.
explain: Because the Earth is tilted, places far from the equator have long summer days and short winter days: in London the Sun sets at about 9:20 pm in late June and about 3:50 pm in late December. Near the poles the Sun can stay up for weeks in summer, while in Singapore, near the equator, day and night are each about 12 hours all year.
explain: Early risers are "larks", like the birds that sing at dawn, and people who stay up late are "night owls"; Australians say "up at sparrow's" for up at first light.
fact: The Earth spins once on its axis about every 24 hours.
fact: The side of the Earth facing the Sun has day, and the side facing away has night.
fact: At midday the Sun is highest and shadows are shortest; in the morning and evening the Sun is low and shadows are long.
fact: A sundial tells the time by the shadow of a stick called a gnomon, which moves round as the Earth turns.
fact: A sundial shows Sun time; in British summer time clocks are 1 hour ahead of it, so 1 pm on a sundial is 2 pm on a watch.
fact: A sundial does not work on a cloudy day or at night, because there is no shadow.
fact: The Earth's axis is tilted, so away from the equator summer days are long and winter days are short.
fact: In London the Sun sets at about 9:20 pm in late June and about 3:50 pm in late December, about 5 and a half hours earlier.
fact: In the far north, as in northern Norway, the Sun does not set for weeks in summer (the midnight Sun) and does not rise for weeks in winter (the polar night).
fact: Near the equator, as in Singapore, day and night are each about 12 hours long all year.
fact: In Britain dawn can be before 5 am in summer and after 7:30 am in winter.
fact: "Up with the larks" means up very early, a "night owl" stays up late, and in Australia "sparrow's" means very early, at first light.
exercise: Why does the Sun seem to disappear at night? | Our side of the Earth turns away from it | The Sun goes and hides behind the Moon at night | The Sun goes out like a lamp until the morning | The Sun sinks down into the sea every evening
exercise: About how long does the Earth take to spin once? | About 24 hours | About 12 hours | About one week | About 365 days
exercise: When is your shadow shortest on a sunny day? | Around midday | Early in the morning | Late in the evening | Just after sunrise
exercise: Why is Josh's shadow long at 6 pm in summer? | The Sun is low in the sky | The Sun is higher than at noon | The Sun is closer to the Earth | The Sun is bigger in the evening
exercise: What is the stick on a sundial called? | A gnomon | A pendulum | A meridian | A hand
exercise: Ellie reads her sundial at 10 am and again at 2 pm. How many hours apart are the readings? | 4 hours | 2 hours | 8 hours | 12 hours
exercise: Why does the shadow on a sundial move round? | The Earth turns, so the Sun seems to cross the sky | The stick slowly bends in the wind | The ground under the sundial slides along | The Sun travels all the way round a still Earth each day
exercise: In British summer time, a sundial says 1 pm. What does a watch say? | 2 pm | 12 noon | 1 pm | 3 pm
exercise: Why does a sundial stop working on a cloudy day? | No sunshine means no shadow | Clouds make the stick too wet | The Sun switches off when it's cloudy | Cold air slows the shadow down
exercise: Why is it still light at 9 pm in June in London? | The Earth's tilt gives the north long summer days | The Sun is much closer to the Earth in June, so it's lighter | The Sun moves more slowly in June | The Moon gives extra light in June
exercise: About what time does the Sun set in London in late December? | About 3:50 pm | About 9:20 pm | About 7 pm | About midnight
exercise: What is the "midnight Sun"? | The Sun staying up all night in the far north in summer | A full moon bright enough to look like the Sun | The Sun rising at exactly midnight in winter | A star that shines only at midnight
exercise: Why is sunset in Singapore around 7 pm all year? | It's near the equator, where day length hardly changes | Singapore changes its clocks every month | Singapore has no winter at all because it is a small island | Singapore is closer to the Sun than Scotland
exercise: What does "up with the larks" mean? | Up very early | Up very late | Up a tall tree | Up in a plane
exercise: What does an Australian mean by "we'll be up at sparrow's"? | We'll get up very early, at first light | We'll feed the sparrows and other birds in the garden | We'll stay up very late, long into the night | We'll go to sleep straight after a big lunch
test: Mila's brother: "The Sun goes to bed, duh." ("Duh" means that's obvious.) Dad: "Nah, we're the ones turning." Who's right? | Dad: the Earth spins and our side turns away from the Sun | The brother: the Sun goes down under the ground | Both: the Sun and the Earth swap places at night | Neither: the Moon covers the Sun at night
test: Josh at 6 pm: "Bruh, my shadow's grown, I'm a giant now!" ("Bruh" means mate.) What really happened? | The Sun is low, so his shadow is long | Josh grew taller since lunchtime | The ground slopes more in the evening | Shadows grow bigger all day long
test: Ollie's mate, in Leeds in July: "Your sundial's busted, mate, it says 1 and my watch says 2." What's going on? | Summer time puts clocks 1 hour ahead of Sun time | The sundial is broken and needs a new stick | The watch is broken and needs a new battery | Sundials always run exactly one hour slow, everywhere on Earth
test: Leo kicks his sundial on a grey day: "Useless piece of junk!" What's true? | It's fine, but it works only when the Sun makes a shadow | It's broken for good now and will never work again, so bin it | It works in the rain but not in fog | It works even better on cloudy days
test: Jade, in London: "Why's it pitch black at half four in December when it's light at 9 in June?" | The Earth's tilt makes northern winter days short and summer days long | The Sun is further away in winter, so it's dimmer | The clocks go back 5 hours every winter | The Sun takes a winter break in December
test: Freya, from south London: "My cousin in Norway plays footy at midnight in June, bare light out there." ("Bare" means loads of.) Is that possible? | Yes, in the far north the Sun doesn't set for weeks in summer | No, the Sun sets by 10 pm everywhere in the world | Only if there is a full moon that night | No, Norway has 12 hours of night all year
test: Nathan's friend in Glasgow: "Sunset at 7 every day of the year? Dinnae talk mince." ("Dinnae talk mince" is Scots for don't talk rubbish.) Who's right? | Nathan: near the equator day length hardly changes | The friend: sunset changes by hours everywhere | The friend: Singapore never has a sunset | Nathan: but only because Singapore is so very small and flat
test: What would happen if the Earth stopped spinning but still went round the Sun? | Each place would get months of day, then months of night | Day and night would stay exactly the same as they are now | It would be daytime everywhere at once | Sundial shadows would move faster than now
test: Ava's gran, at 5 am: "Up with the larks, are we?" What does she mean? | You're up very early | You've been out birdwatching | You stayed up all night | You're as noisy as a bird
test: A cartoon shows the Sun on a little track, driving round the Earth each day. Put it right. | The Earth spins, and that makes the Sun seem to cross the sky | The Sun really drives round, but much faster | The Earth goes round the Sun once every day | The Sun and the Earth both stand still
test: Kofi's dad: "Up at sparrow's, mate, no whinging." ("Whinging" means moaning.) Kofi: "What's a sparrow got to do with it?" Best answer? | "Sparrow's" means first light, when birds start to sing | It means feeding the sparrows before school | It means getting up as soon as a sparrow lands on your roof | It means sleeping in late like a lazy bird
exam: Identify the principal reason for the alternation of day and night. | The rotation of the Earth upon its axis approximately once in twenty-four hours | The revolution of the Sun about a stationary Earth once daily | The periodic obscuring of the Sun by the Moon | The diminution of the Sun's light at fixed hours
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the length of shadows. | Shadows are shortest when the Sun attains its greatest height, about midday | Shadows are longest when the Sun attains its greatest height | Shadows are of constant length throughout the day | Shadows are shortest at sunrise and at sunset
exam: Given that British Summer Time is in force, ascertain the time indicated by a watch when a sundial indicates one o'clock in the afternoon. | Two o'clock in the afternoon | Twelve o'clock noon | One o'clock in the afternoon | Three o'clock in the afternoon
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the duration of daylight at the equator? | Day and night are each approximately twelve hours throughout the year | Daylight persists for several weeks during the summer | Daylight is absent for several weeks during the winter | Daylight varies by some five hours between summer and winter
